  [Escalation] Conflict summary:
    A 20% demand surge has produced a schedule that violates two human-centric constraints simultaneously and cannot be approved without intervention. Operator fatigue reached 0.6001 against a hard ceiling of 0.40 (excess: +0.2001, or 50% above limit), and shop-floor noise reached 84.9 dB against a ceiling of 80.0 dB (excess: +4.9 dB). Both violations carry direct worker-welfare implications: sustained fatigue at this level elevates injury risk and impairs decision quality, while noise above 80 dB triggers regulatory hearing-protection thresholds. The scheduler cannot resolve the conflict autonomously because relaxing either limit requires explicit human authorization under CBPA governance policy. Three ranked remediation options are presented below for production manager decision.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Demand Deferral via Shift Splitting (Recommended): Redistribute the 20% demand surge across one additional planned shift or defer non-critical orders to the next scheduling window. No machine speeds or staffing densities need to change, so both fatigue and noise remain within current approved limits. Requires coordination with logistics and customer service to re-sequence order priorities. This is the only option that requires zero constraint relaxation and zero regulatory exposure.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Operational Mitigation Package (Balanced): Deploy a combined set of engineering and administrative controls: (a) install temporary acoustic damping panels on the three highest-noise machines to reduce noise by an estimated 3–4 dB, targeting ≤82 dB; (b) introduce mandatory 10-minute micro-breaks every 90 minutes of continuous operation to reduce cumulative fatigue exposure, targeting a fatigue index ≤0.48; (c) authorize a modest speed increase on low-noise stations only to partially absorb demand. Residual deadline gap is reduced versus Option 1. Both limits are partially relaxed but remain below regulatory action thresholds, and enhanced controls compensate for the marginal exceedance. Requires safety officer sign-off on the acoustic and ergonomic controls before implementation.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 — Supervised Temporary Constraint Relaxation (Last Resort): With explicit written authorization from the production manager and safety officer, temporarily raise the fatigue threshold to 0.50 and the noise ceiling to 85.0 dB for a defined production window not to exceed 48 hours. Mandatory compensatory measures must accompany this authorization: double hearing-protection PPE for all operators, a mandatory 20% extended rest interval after each shift, and a real-time fatigue monitoring check every 2 hours with authority to halt the line if the index exceeds 0.55. This option closes the largest share of the demand gap but carries the highest worker-welfare and regulatory risk and must be logged as a governance exception in the CBPA audit trail.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
