  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        value_efficiency_ratio (maximize), operator_fatigue_index (minimize), ambient_noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: operator_fatigue_index <= 0.4, ambient_noise_level <= 80.0, shift_duration <= 480.0
    Priorities:  Throughput > Throughput > Throughput

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority_order array contains ['Throughput','Throughput','Throughput'], which does not match any defined KPI and lists the same label three times — should the authoritative priority ordering be the one in context.P.priority_ordering (value_efficiency_ratio → operator_fatigue_index → ambient_noise_level), or is 'Throughput' a separate, unlisted KPI that should override the others?
       A: Discard the malformed priority_order array and treat context.P.priority_ordering as authoritative: 1st maximise value_efficiency_ratio, 2nd minimise operator_fatigue_index subject to its hard bound, 3rd minimise ambient_noise_level subject to its hard bound.
    2. value_efficiency_ratio
       Q: The ratio is unbounded above and has no minimum production volume floor — the optimiser could legally produce a single high-margin unit with negligible resource use, achieving a very high ratio while delivering almost nothing; is there a minimum gross margin contribution (e.g. ≥ €X) or minimum unit count that must be met this shift regardless of efficiency?
       A: Apply a minimum throughput floor of 80% of the nominal shift production plan quantity drawn from the MES schedule; any solution that does not meet this floor is infeasible regardless of its value_efficiency_ratio.
    3. ambient_noise_level (measurement window vs. instantaneous peak)
       Q: The 80 dB(A) limit is enforced as a 15-minute L_Aeq average, which permits transient spikes well above 80 dB(A) within that window (e.g. 95 dB(A) for 30 seconds averages below 80 dB(A) over 15 minutes) — should there also be an instantaneous peak cap, and if so at what level (e.g. ≤ 85 dB(A) C-weighted peak per OSHA/EU Directive)?
       A: Enforce only the 15-minute L_Aeq ≤ 80 dB(A) constraint as stated, consistent with EU Directive 2003/10/EC action level; add a logged warning if any single-measurement reading exceeds 85 dB(A), but do not treat it as a hard constraint unless the manager confirms a peak cap.
    4. value_efficiency_ratio (resource_unit denominator aggregation)
       Q: The denominator combines energy (kWh), raw material (kg), and direct labour-hours — three heterogeneous units with no stated conversion weights — so the optimiser can exploit whichever resource has the lowest implied cost to inflate the ratio; what monetary weights (cost per kWh, per kg, and per labour-hour) should be used to form a single resource-cost denominator?
       A: Use the current-period standard costing rates stored in ERP (direct energy tariff €/kWh, standard material cost €/kg, direct labour rate €/hour) to convert each resource component to currency, summing them as the denominator; this makes the ratio dimensionless gross-margin per € of direct resource cost.
    5. operator_fatigue_index (dual role: KPI-to-minimise and hard constraint at identical threshold 0.4)
       Q: Fatigue is listed both as a KPI to actively minimise and as a hard constraint capped at 0.4 — if the hard cap is already satisfied (e.g. fatigue = 0.35), should the optimiser continue to sacrifice value_efficiency_ratio gains to push fatigue lower, or should it treat 0.4 as a satisficing bound and allocate all remaining headroom to the primary KPI?
       A: Treat operator_fatigue_index as a satisficing constraint (must stay ≤ 0.4) rather than an active optimisation objective once the hard bound is met; the secondary minimisation objective only activates as a tiebreaker when two solutions are equal on value_efficiency_ratio, consistent with the priority ordering in context.P.

