  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        resource_efficiency (maximize), operator_fatigue (upper_bound), noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: operator_fatigue <= 0.4, noise_level <= 80.0
    Priorities:  Throughput > Throughput > Throughput

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority list contains 'Throughput' three times and does not reference any of the three KPIs in this contract (resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, noise_level); should the priority order be resource_efficiency > operator_fatigue > noise_level, and is raw throughput a separate objective or a proxy for efficiency?
       A: Priority order is set to resource_efficiency (primary) > operator_fatigue (secondary) > noise_level (tertiary); raw throughput is treated as a sub-component of resource_efficiency, not an independent objective.
    2. resource_efficiency (maximize, threshold: null)
       Q: Maximising resource_efficiency with no saturation point or minimum-gain floor allows the optimiser to push machine_speed and production_rate to their physical limits, potentially raising energy draw by ~30-40% and noise risk; is there a maximum acceptable machine utilisation rate (e.g. ≤ 90%) or a minimum efficiency improvement target (e.g. +10% vs. baseline) beyond which further gains are not worth the added stress?
       A: Machine utilisation is capped at 90% of rated capacity and a minimum efficiency gain of +5% over the shift baseline is used as the saturation threshold; gains beyond that are not penalised but not actively pursued if they risk constraint violations.
    3. noise_level (threshold: 80 dB, averaged)
       Q: The assumption specifies a time-averaged A-weighted level; this allows the optimiser to schedule short high-speed bursts (potentially 88-92 dB) offset by quiet periods to stay at 80 dB average, which may still damage hearing and violate OSHA short-duration exposure limits—should 80 dB be enforced as a continuous ceiling (no instantaneous exceedance) rather than a shift average?
       A: 80 dB is enforced as a rolling 15-minute Leq ceiling; no measurement window during the shift may exceed 80 dB, preventing burst-and-recover exploitation while preserving the shift-average intent.
    4. operator_fatigue (threshold: 0.4, temporal interpretation)
       Q: The fatigue model is described as a 'cumulative workload index over shift duration', but it is unclear whether 0.4 is a limit that must not be exceeded at any instant during the shift or only at shift end; if it is end-of-shift only, the optimiser may legitimately allow fatigue to spike above 0.4 mid-shift and then recover via rest_scheduling—which interpretation is intended?
       A: 0.4 is enforced as a never-exceed ceiling at every scheduling epoch (e.g. every 30-minute interval), not merely at shift end, to protect against sustained mid-shift overload.
    5. delivered_value_metric (variant mix freedom)
       Q: The delivered_value metric weights output by 'product value or quality grade', so if the variant mix is a free lever the optimiser may shift all production to the single highest-value, lowest-resource SKU, possibly violating customer order commitments—is the variant mix fixed by the production schedule, or is re-mixing an allowed optimisation lever, and if so within what bounds (e.g. ±15% of planned quantities per variant)?
       A: Variant mix is treated as fixed by the existing production schedule; the optimiser may not deviate from planned per-variant quantities by more than ±10%, and delivered value is computed against those fixed quantities.

