    3. operator_fatigue (preferred operating level vs. hard-limit boundary)
       Q: The fatigue hard constraint is 0.4, but with 'maximise efficiency' as the primary KPI the optimiser will likely hold fatigue near 0.4 for most of the shift. Is operating continuously at 0.4 acceptable, or is there a preferred ceiling (e.g. 0.30) below which the schedule should stay unless efficiency gains are substantial (say, ≥ 10%)?
       A: A soft preferred ceiling of 0.30 is applied with a penalty weight; the optimiser may exceed 0.30 up to the hard limit of 0.40 only when the efficiency gain is ≥ 5%. This prevents the optimiser from saturating the fatigue constraint as its default operating point.
    4. noise_level (time-averaged vs. instantaneous ceiling)
       Q: The noise constraint is '≤ 80 dBA' measured as A-weighted time-averaged SPL, but 80 dBA is also the EU Directive 2003/10/EC daily exposure action level. Should 80 dBA be treated as an instantaneous ceiling (no moment may exceed it), a rolling 15-minute average, or a full-shift TWA — since machines that spike to 88 dBA for short bursts can still comply with a shift TWA below 80 dBA?
       A: 80 dBA is treated as a 15-minute rolling average ceiling measured at operator ear level, consistent with ISO 9612 short-period method. Instantaneous peaks above 85 dBA trigger a separate flag but do not violate the constraint unless sustained beyond one measurement interval.
    5. delivered_value_per_resource (value_metric quality-adjustment formula undefined)
       Q: The value metric is 'throughput_quality_adjusted_output', but the quality-adjustment formula is not specified. If the optimiser can run at a faster cycle rate that increases scrap from 2% to 8%, does it still count the scrapped units as delivered value, and by how much is the per-unit value discounted — since without this formula the optimiser may game throughput at the expense of yield?
       A: Only units meeting the current quality acceptance criteria count as delivered value (binary pass/fail). Scrap and rework units contribute zero value. The scrap rate from the previous shift baseline is used as the reference; any schedule that is projected to increase scrap above baseline by more than 2 percentage points is flagged for review before execution.

