    A 20.0% demand surge has produced a schedule that violates two human-centric constraints and has been automatically rejected. Operator fatigue reached 0.5165 against a hard ceiling of 0.40 (excess: +0.1165, ~29% over limit), and shop-floor noise reached 83.3 dB against a hard ceiling of 80.0 dB (excess: +3.3 dB, ~4% over limit). Because both violations concern worker health and safety, the CBPA framework cannot autonomously approve a waiver and requires production manager authorization before any remediation path is executed. No production has been scheduled for the affected window. Three ranked options follow, ordered from most to least protective of human-centric constraints.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Add a Supplementary Shift (Recommended): Redistribute the 20% demand increase across a second production shift (or extend an existing shift with a fresh crew rotation). Current operators work only their standard allocation; the incremental volume is absorbed by additional staffing. This eliminates both violations without touching any constraint threshold. Requires HR authorization for shift expansion and, if applicable, temporary-labour procurement lead time of 24–72 hours.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Partial Demand Fulfillment (Constraint-Safe Throughput Cap): Cap accepted demand at the maximum throughput level that keeps operator fatigue at or below 0.40 and noise below 80.0 dB. Based on the current violation margins, the feasible demand uplift is approximately 8% rather than 20%, leaving roughly 12% of the incremental demand unfulfilled in this scheduling window. The unmet volume should be deferred to a future shift or negotiated with the customer as a short delivery extension. No constraint thresholds are modified; all human-centric protections remain fully intact.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Temporary Constraint Relaxation with Enhanced Safety Controls (Last Resort): With explicit production manager sign-off and documented risk acceptance, temporarily raise the operator fatigue ceiling to 0.52 (covering the observed 0.5165 with a 0.005 buffer) and the noise ceiling to 84.0 dB (covering the observed 83.3 dB with a 0.7 dB buffer). Mandatory mitigations must accompany this waiver: (a) mandatory 15-minute micro-rest breaks every 90 minutes for all operators, (b) mandatory ISO-rated hearing protection (SNR ≥ 25 dB) for all personnel in the noise zone, (c) continuous real-time fatigue and noise monitoring with auto-halt if either threshold is breached, and (d) a post-shift welfare check. This option fully meets the 20% demand target but formally reduces human-constraint protection for the duration of the waiver window. The waiver must be re-evaluated each shift and cannot be extended beyond 72 hours without a secondary review.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
