  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Expand Labor Capacity via Supplemental Shift (Recommended): Authorize a third rotating shift or bring in cross-trained supplemental operators to redistribute the 25% demand increase across a larger workforce. Simultaneously deploy targeted acoustic damping measures (machine enclosures, anti-vibration mounts) on the highest-noise workstations identified in the noise audit. This eliminates both violations without relaxing any constraint and fully absorbs the demand surge. Lead time: 1–2 shifts to staff and brief new operators. Minor schedule delay expected while staffing is confirmed.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Partial Demand Deferral (~10% to Next Planning Cycle): Defer approximately 10% of the incremental demand (equivalent to ~8% of total baseline volume) to the next planning window, reducing effective load to a level that brings operator fatigue back below 0.40 and noise below 80 dB within the existing shift structure. Coordinate with the commercial team to identify lowest-priority SKUs or orders with schedule flexibility. This preserves all human-centric constraints and requires no capital outlay, but introduces a partial delivery shortfall this cycle.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Temporary Controlled Constraint Relaxation with Compensatory Controls (Last Resort): With explicit production manager sign-off and documented risk acceptance, temporarily raise the operator fatigue ceiling to 0.52 and the noise limit to 85 dB for a defined period (not to exceed 5 consecutive working days). Mandatory compensatory controls must be activated immediately: (a) issue EN ISO 4869-compliant hearing protection rated ≥10 dB attenuation to all floor personnel, (b) enforce mandatory 15-minute rest breaks every 90 minutes to partially offset fatigue accumulation, (c) activate continuous real-time monitoring with automatic line-stop triggers if either parameter exceeds the relaxed ceiling. This option meets full demand but formally violates the framework's human-welfare constraints and requires post-period medical follow-up and regulatory disclosure.
      Preserves human constraints: No

