  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        resource_efficiency (maximize), operator_fatigue (upper_bound), ambient_noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: operator_fatigue <= 0.4, ambient_noise_level <= 80.0
    Priorities:  Throughput > Throughput > Throughput

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority list contains 'Throughput' three times but the contract's KPIs are resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, and ambient_noise_level — should the ordering be [resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, ambient_noise_level], and is fatigue or noise the higher-priority guard-rail when they conflict with each other?
       A: Priority order set to [resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, ambient_noise_level]; fatigue treated as the higher-priority secondary constraint because ergonomic harm is harder to reverse than a short noise exceedance.
    2. resource_efficiency (no throughput floor)
       Q: Maximising value-per-resource-unit with no minimum output floor allows the optimiser to satisfy the objective by producing a very small quantity of high-margin items; is there a minimum acceptable output volume (e.g. ≥ N units or ≥ £X delivered value) that must be met within the shift regardless of efficiency gains?
       A: A soft throughput floor of 80% of the historical shift average is applied; the optimiser may not reduce volume below this floor even if doing so would increase the efficiency ratio.
    3. resource_efficiency (resource input weighting)
       Q: The denominator aggregates energy (kWh), raw material (kg), and labor-hours into a single 'resource_units' figure — what cost or conversion weights should be used to combine them (e.g. £0.18/kWh, £1.20/kg material, £22/labor-hour), or should one input category be held fixed while the others are optimised?
       A: Monetary cost weights derived from the site's last-month actuals are used (energy, material, labor); if unavailable, equal normalised weights are applied and a warning is logged for manager review before the schedule is released.
    4. operator_fatigue (peak vs. time-averaged enforcement)
       Q: The fatigue limit of 0.4 is labelled 'time-averaged over the shift' in the assumptions, but a time-average allows instantaneous spikes well above 0.4 — should the 0.4 threshold be enforced as a continuous peak-at-any-moment cap, or as a rolling-window average (e.g. any 30-minute window), to prevent short but harmful overload episodes?
       A: The 0.4 threshold is enforced as a rolling 30-minute peak: fatigue must not exceed 0.4 in any 30-minute window, which is stricter than a shift average but less restrictive than an instantaneous cap.
    5. ambient_noise_level (measurement scope — single vs. multi-station)
       Q: The noise constraint (≤ 80 dB Leq) is measured 'at the operator workstation' — if multiple operators work at different stations with different noise exposures, does the 80 dB limit apply independently at every occupied station, or as an average across all stations, and are noise-intensive operations permitted during scheduled breaks when no operators are present?
       A: The 80 dB Leq constraint applies independently at every occupied operator workstation; noise-intensive tasks may be scheduled during operator breaks (station unoccupied) without violating the constraint, provided the post-break Leq resets within the first 10 minutes after operators return.

