======================================================================
  PHASE 1: Intent Translation & Contract Deployment
======================================================================

  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        resource_efficiency (maximize), operator_fatigue (upper_bound), noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: operator_fatigue <= 0.4, noise_level <= 80.0
    Priorities:  Throughput > Throughput > Throughput

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority order lists 'Throughput' three times, but no Throughput KPI is defined in the contract — should the priority order instead reflect the three declared KPIs (e.g., resource_efficiency > operator_fatigue > noise_level), and is there a minimum production volume the shift must deliver regardless of efficiency gains?
       A: Priority order is reassigned to [resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, noise_level] in that rank; no minimum throughput floor is enforced unless the site production plan specifies one.
    2. resource_efficiency / resource_inputs_include
       Q: The efficiency denominator aggregates energy_kWh, raw_material_kg, labor_hours, and machine_time_hours into a single 'resource_units' figure — what monetary or normalised weighting should each input carry (e.g., energy at £0.12/kWh, labour at £18/hr), since without weights the optimiser can exploit whichever input is cheapest to game the ratio?
       A: Each resource type is converted to cost using site-standard unit prices from the ERP system; if unavailable, equal normalised weights (each input scaled to its shift-level maximum) are applied.
    3. resource_efficiency / upper bound on individual resource consumption
       Q: Maximising the efficiency ratio has no cap on absolute resource draw — if the optimiser raises machine utilisation to ~95 % to increase output value, energy consumption may rise ~35–40 % above baseline; is there an absolute energy budget (kWh) or machine-utilisation ceiling (%) that must not be exceeded during this shift?
       A: Machine utilisation is capped at 85 % and energy consumption at 110 % of the shift's historical median; fatigue and noise constraints provide the only other upper limits.
    4. operator_fatigue / soft preference vs. hard constraint threshold alignment
       Q: Both the KPI soft target and the hard constraint are set to the same value (0.4 index); this means the optimiser has no incentive to stay below the safety limit until it is already breached — should the soft optimisation target be set lower (e.g., ≤ 0.30) to preserve a buffer before the regulatory hard stop at 0.40?
       A: The soft KPI optimisation target is lowered to 0.30 while the hard constraint remains at 0.40, creating a 0.10-index safety buffer for the solver.
    5. noise_level / measurement scope
       Q: The 80 dB limit is measured at a single operator workstation (LAeq shift-average), but if multiple workstations exist or workers rotate positions, which measurement point governs — the worst-case station, the average across all stations, or the station of the highest-risk operator?
       A: The constraint applies to the maximum LAeq recorded across all occupied workstations during the shift; if real-time monitoring covers only one point, that point is treated as representative.

