  [Escalation] Conflict summary:
    A 15.0% demand increase has produced a schedule that violates two human-centric constraints and has been rejected by the CBPA framework. Operator fatigue reached 0.5322 against a ceiling of 0.40 (overage: +0.1322), and floor noise reached 84.0 dB against a ceiling of 80.0 dB (overage: +4.0 dB). Both constraints are safety-critical: sustained fatigue above threshold elevates injury risk and reduces quality, while prolonged noise above 80 dB exceeds recommended occupational exposure limits. No feasible schedule currently exists that satisfies full demand without breaching at least one of these limits. Human escalation is required before production can proceed.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Staggered Workforce Expansion (Fully Compliant): Add one additional shift rotation and cross-train available staff to distribute the incremental demand load across more operators. Fatigue per operator is brought back within the 0.40 ceiling by reducing consecutive work blocks. Acoustic load is similarly redistributed across longer low-intensity windows, keeping noise at or below 80 dB. Full demand cannot be met within the original deadline horizon; approximately 7–8% of the incremental volume would be deferred to the next planning window. This is the only option that requires no constraint relaxation and carries no safety trade-off.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Engineering Controls with Selective Noise-Limit Adjustment: Deploy temporary acoustic enclosures and mandatory PPE (earmuffs rated ≥ NRR 25) on the three highest-noise workstations, then formally relax the operational noise ceiling to 84.0 dB for the duration of the surge period under an approved hearing-conservation addendum. Operator fatigue is held at the 0.40 limit by capping shift extensions to 30 minutes maximum and mandating a 15-minute recovery break per 2-hour block. This satisfies the fatigue constraint entirely but requires documented managerial sign-off to temporarily raise the noise limit. Approximately 2% of demand may still slip past the deadline due to break scheduling overhead.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 — Full Demand Acceptance with Dual-Constraint Override (Escalation Required): Accept the complete 15% demand increase by running the rejected schedule as-is, with operator fatigue at 0.5322 and noise at 84.0 dB. This eliminates the deadline gap entirely but requires formal written override authorization from both the production manager and the occupational health and safety officer. A real-time fatigue monitoring protocol (biometric or checklist-based) and hourly noise dosimetry checks must be activated as compensating controls. This option carries elevated regulatory and liability exposure and is recommended only if customer penalty costs exceed the estimated risk-adjustment cost of safety non-compliance. All override records must be logged in the CBPA audit trail for post-surge review.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
