    - Option 1 (Recommended) — Expand Labour Capacity via Shift Rotation: Add a second operator rotation pool by introducing a split-shift or cross-trained relief team, distributing workload across more personnel. This directly targets the fatigue root cause by reducing continuous exposure time per operator to well below the 0.40 threshold. Pair with scheduled acoustic rest zones to keep noise exposure within the 80 dB limit. No constraint limits are relaxed; demand is met through additional human capacity. Requires HR coordination and may incur overtime or temporary-hire costs.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate) — Acoustic Engineering Controls with Minor Noise Limit Adjustment: Deploy portable acoustic dampening barriers and mandate enhanced PPE (noise-cancelling ear protection rated for industrial environments) across all affected workstations. Re-schedule the highest-noise operations to off-peak hours with staggered operator assignments to reduce cumulative fatigue. A time-boxed relaxation of the noise limit to 82.0 dB may be approved for up to 30 days while permanent acoustic fixtures are procured and installed. Fatigue constraint is preserved at 0.40. Achieves approximately 85-90% of demand target within the current deadline window.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 (Last Resort) — Partial Demand Deferral with Time-Boxed Dual Constraint Relaxation: Defer approximately 15% of the demand increment to the next planning horizon and negotiate a revised delivery schedule with downstream stakeholders. For the remaining demand, approve a short-term, board-ratified relaxation of both constraints: fatigue ceiling raised to 0.45 (from 0.40) and noise ceiling raised to 82.5 dB (from 80.0 dB), with mandatory enhanced PPE, fatigue monitoring via wearable sensors, and a 72-hour review checkpoint. All relaxations expire automatically after 14 calendar days and must be renewed with fresh medical clearance. Compensatory rest days must be scheduled within the same pay period.
      Preserves human constraints: No

