       A: Assume a start-of-shift baseline fatigue of 0.25 (CBPA framework default for a rested operator); the 0.4 hard ceiling is preserved with no relaxation. If this assumption proves infeasible at runtime, the optimiser should report an infeasibility warning rather than silently violating the constraint.
    5. noise_level — measurement method (time-averaged vs peak)
       Q: The 80 dB limit is described as 'time-averaged over the full shift', but regulatory standards (e.g. OSHA, ISO 9612) typically also impose instantaneous peak limits (e.g. 140 dB C-weighted) — should the 80 dB ceiling apply as a shift-wide Leq (equivalent continuous level), as a never-exceed instantaneous cap, or both?
       A: Enforce 80 dB as a shift-wide A-weighted Leq (time-averaged SPL); additionally apply a hard instantaneous cap of 110 dB peak (ISO 9612 action level) to prevent short high-intensity bursts from being masked by the average.

